{
 "name": "quadratic",
 "category": "quadratic",
 "source": "Biq Mac Library (A. Wiegele, Alpen-Adria-Universitaet Klagenfurt), https://biqmac.aau.at/biqmaclib.html: the quadratic 0-1 part 'Biq: min x'Qx s.t. x in {0,1}^n' with its three sets beasley (Beasley 1998, bqp<n>-<i>), gka (Glover, Kochenberger and Alidaee 1998, sets a to f) and be (Billionnet and Elloumi 2007), in the sparse format of the page; and the library's description biqmaclib.tex (September 2007), which states the problem and gives in its tables the optimal values, or bounds where the optimum is not known. Only the instances with a stated optimal value are fetched: bqp50, bqp100, bqp250 (10 each), gka sets a to e (40), the 8 be classes (80); left out and not fetched: bqp500-1..10 and gka1f..gka5f (the tables give an upper bound and a lower bound only), bqp1000-* and bqp2500-* (served in the folder, absent from the tables), and the .mat files (the same matrices in full form; the sparse form is the only one served for every set; the .mat files of the 120 gka and be instances used were compared once, outside the adapter, with the symmetric completion of their sparse files and are equal)",
 "terms": "The Biq Mac pages state no licence or terms of use. The page biqmaclib.html says: 'This site offers a collection of Max-Cut instances and quadratic 0-1 programming problems of medium size ... The instances are mainly ment to be used for testing exact solution methods for quadratic 0-1 programming or Max-Cut problems'; of the beasley sets: 'The datasets can be obtained from this site or from the OR-Library'; of the gka sets: 'Also these datasets can be obtained from this website or from the OR-Library'. Fetched by hash, parsed as text, never executed, not redistributed by this package",
 "reference": "the optimal values printed in the tables of biqmaclib.tex (pinned as a data file and parsed; kernel only), in the library's convention: values of min x'Qx for the matrices of the library's files (the OR-Library states the beasley and gka problems as maximisation; the library's values are the minima of its own files). Values with '\\le' are bounds and are not used. The source publishes values only, no solution vectors",
